# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Client environment (Vite)
#
# Everything here is inlined into the browser bundle and readable by anyone who
# loads the app. That is fine for the Firebase web config — it is not a secret,
# and Firestore security rules are what actually protect the data.
#
# Nothing secret belongs in this file. Secrets live in Cloud Functions; see
# functions/.env.example.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Firebase web config — Project settings › Your apps › SDK setup and configuration.
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=your_api_key_here
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=your_project.firebaseapp.com
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your_project_id
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=your_project.appspot.com
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=your_sender_id
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=your_app_id

# NOTE: there is deliberately no VITE_OPENAI_API_KEY and no VITE_STRIPE_* here.
# Slip scanning and checkout both run through Cloud Functions now, so the OpenAI
# key and the Stripe secret key never reach the browser. If you find yourself
# adding a secret with a VITE_ prefix, it belongs in functions/ instead.
